 "voice": "af_heart",
 "speed": 1.0,
 "text": "Every scene in this tool is written by an AI agent. So how do we know a change makes it better, and not just different? We start with a gold set: real scenes, with their inputs frozen. Every change runs the harness. Agents rebuild each case, several times. A deterministic gate checks the basics: it compiles, lint is clean, the seams line up. Then a judge scores the rest. It's a free, local model, asked both ways round, to cancel its bias. But a judge only earns trust when it agrees with people. We label outputs blind, and measure the agreement, criterion by criterion. Here's a real experiment. The agents' top complaint was drawing every arrow by hand. We added lists and curves, and wrote the decision rule down first. Turns per section dropped from twenty-four to nineteen. Cost fell sixteen percent. Quality held, so we accepted it. After release, the loop keeps turning. Every generation is traced. Monitors alert an owner. We analyze the failures, propose a fix, and ship it only if the evals pass. And the hardest real cases become new gold, so the test set grows with the app.",
